From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DrummingBird1 <1979036+DrummingBird1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:11:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] D3D12: guest shaders through a bytecode cache on disk

D3DCompile is where a new guest program costs its frame. FXC spends half
a second on a skinned vertex program of 90 instructions, and a scene cut
that brings a few in stopped Sonic the Hedgehog's story scene for 516 ms
(97% of the stall's samples in D3DCOMPILER_47 under vp_get_vs). Optimisation
levels and reshaping the generated HLSL (register arrays as variables, a
plain multiply) took at most a third off.

shader_compile() keeps what it compiles: <hash of HLSL, target, flags>.dxbc
in D3D12_SHADER_CACHE (default: shadercache next to the exe; 0 = off),
written to a temporary name and renamed, and read back only as a whole DXBC
container. Vertex and fragment programs go through it; repeats inside a run
(the same fragment program for another pipeline) are reads too. FXC now
skips its optimiser for guest programs (D3D12_SHADER_OPT=skip, the default;
1 is FXC's own): the slowest program compiles 2.5x faster, and the driver
optimises the bytecode again when it builds the pipeline. PS3_FTRACE gets
a span per shader (kind 17), D3D12_SHADER_CACHE_DBG=1 logs each.

Story scene, 45 s: first run 29.81 flips/s with one 17-vblank stall (was 36
with FXC's optimiser); second run 30.00, every one of 1349 frames 2 vblanks.
Wave Ocean at 3840x2160 ran at 60 with either optimisation setting (RTX 4070
SUPER); a weaker card has not been measured.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
